Same LZW and the same output. The reference's dictionary is a HashMap keyed by the whole byte string (hashing a string as long as the current match, about a thousand bytes by the end); here a string in the dictionary is (the code of its prefix, its last byte), the table is keyed by that pair (the standard LZW dictionary, the language has no map type): same codes, a different data structure with constant work per byte, which favors this port. Other stores are range-checked; state is unlogged, so nothing is saved for a rollback. Scratch state rebuilt every run (the reference's locals: codes, keys, values) is not logged (state is unlogged by default, so no undo log, as a Rust local has none).
